WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.578
Begin by framing Course Foundations and Scope
as a question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:08.578 --> 00:00:09.796
observable results.

3
00:00:09.796 --> 00:00:17.781
Real-world datasets are often large and can be
stored in either main memory or a database.

4
00:00:17.781 --> 00:00:25.515
Consider this worked case: Student teams will
load a 100,000-row customer transaction

5
00:00:25.515 --> 00:00:30.671
dataset into a relational database for initial
exploration.

6
00:00:30.671 --> 00:00:37.365
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

7
00:00:37.365 --> 00:00:37.843
conclusion.

8
00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:43.531
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

9
00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:50.372
Now define Knowledge Discovery Process
precisely enough that another learner could

10
00:00:50.372 --> 00:00:52.209
test the definition.

11
00:00:52.209 --> 00:00:59.357
The emphasis is on how to use data mining to
describe datasets rather than making

12
00:00:59.357 --> 00:00:59.834
predictions.

13
00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:06.880
Consider this worked case: Instructors will
demonstrate how clustering customer purchase

14
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.005
patterns reveals market segments without
forecasting future sales.

15
00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:18.582
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

16
00:01:18.582 --> 00:01:20.974
evidence would distinguish them.

17
00:01:20.974 --> 00:01:27.552
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

18
00:01:27.674 --> 00:01:35.095
With the foundation in place, examine how Data
Exploration and Cleaning operates in a

19
00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.155
concrete sequence.

20
00:01:36.155 --> 00:01:43.030
Data quality analysis makes missingness,
duplicates, inconsistent representation,

21
00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:49.045
integrity constraints, sampling, and update
behavior explicit.

22
00:01:49.045 --> 00:01:57.001
Consider this worked case: Students will run a
missingness report on a survey dataset,

23
00:01:57.001 --> 00:02:04.389
flagging 15% null values in the 'income'
column and removing 3% duplicate records.

24
00:02:04.389 --> 00:02:11.827
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

25
00:02:11.827 --> 00:02:17.686
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

26
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:24.578
Next, compare the choices involved in Data
Preparation Workflows and make the decision

27
00:02:24.578 --> 00:02:25.619
criteria explicit.

28
00:02:25.619 --> 00:02:32.771
Data-analysis workflows separate data
preparation, exploratory analysis, training,

29
00:02:32.771 --> 00:02:38.135
validation, testing, model selection, and
evaluation.

30
00:02:38.135 --> 00:02:46.348
Consider this worked case: A pipeline will be
built where raw data undergoes imputation and

31
00:02:46.348 --> 00:02:50.181
normalization before entering the exploratory
analysis phase.

32
00:02:50.181 --> 00:02:57.387
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

33
00:02:57.387 --> 00:02:58.588
the goalposts.

34
00:02:58.588 --> 00:03:05.447
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

35
00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:14.266
Apply Supervised and Unsupervised Methods to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

36
00:03:14.266 --> 00:03:15.426
isolated definition.

37
00:03:15.426 --> 00:03:22.786
Supervised methods learn mappings from
examples paired with target labels, while

38
00:03:22.786 --> 00:03:26.801
unsupervised methods identify structure
without targets.

39
00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:33.582
Consider this worked case: Students will
compare a decision tree trained on labeled

40
00:03:33.582 --> 00:03:40.363
customer churn data against a k-means
clustering run on the same unlabeled features.

41
00:03:40.363 --> 00:03:48.024
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

42
00:03:48.024 --> 00:03:48.535
step.

43
00:03:48.535 --> 00:03:53.707
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

44
00:03:53.810 --> 00:04:00.682
Examine the tradeoffs in Preprocessing and
Leakage Prevention, including what improves

45
00:04:00.682 --> 00:04:03.806
and what becomes more difficult.

46
00:04:03.806 --> 00:04:10.728
Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted
training data to avoid leakage.

47
00:04:10.728 --> 00:04:17.927
Consider this worked case: Instructors will
demonstrate scaling a feature using only the

48
00:04:17.927 --> 00:04:24.572
training set statistics, then applying the
same transformation to the validation set.

49
00:04:24.572 --> 00:04:31.228
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

50
00:04:31.228 --> 00:04:38.681
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

51
00:04:38.786 --> 00:04:46.908
Stress-test the reasoning around Pipelines and
Consistency with an edge case and identify

52
00:04:46.908 --> 00:04:50.032
which assumption controls the outcome.

53
00:04:50.032 --> 00:04:57.724
Pipelines combine transformations and
estimators so the same operations are applied

54
00:04:57.724 --> 00:04:58.423
consistently.

55
00:04:58.423 --> 00:05:05.685
Consider this worked case: A single pipeline
object will chain standardization, imputation,

56
00:05:05.685 --> 00:05:11.736
and clustering, ensuring identical operations
run on every data split.

57
00:05:11.736 --> 00:05:18.235
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

58
00:05:18.235 --> 00:05:25.298
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

59
00:05:25.418 --> 00:05:33.283
Use Cross-Validation and Sampling to diagnose
a failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

60
00:05:33.283 --> 00:05:34.493
corrective action.

61
00:05:34.493 --> 00:05:41.008
Cross-validation repeats training and
validation across defined splits.

62
00:05:41.008 --> 00:05:47.846
Consider this worked case: Students will
execute a 5-fold cross-validation routine,

63
00:05:47.846 --> 00:05:54.683
rotating the validation set through each
dataset partition and recording accuracy

64
00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:55.305
scores.

65
00:05:55.305 --> 00:06:02.755
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

66
00:06:02.755 --> 00:06:04.352
smallest plausible cause.

67
00:06:04.352 --> 00:06:12.117
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

68
00:06:12.218 --> 00:06:19.094
Connect Algorithm Selection and Evaluation to
the earlier ideas and explain how the pieces

69
00:06:19.094 --> 00:06:21.058
work as one system.

70
00:06:21.058 --> 00:06:26.247
Grouping, dimensionality reduction,
classification, regression, and clustering

71
00:06:26.247 --> 00:06:32.918
answer different questions and require
appropriate assumptions and metrics.

72
00:06:32.918 --> 00:06:39.875
Consider this worked case: Instructors will
match a regression model to predicting

73
00:06:39.875 --> 00:06:46.831
continuous revenue and a clustering algorithm
to segment user behavior, explaining the

74
00:06:46.831 --> 00:06:49.730
distinct evaluation metrics for each.

75
00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:55.355
g. , RMSE, silhouette score) attached to each
leaf.

76
00:06:55.355 --> 00:07:04.944
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

77
00:07:04.944 --> 00:07:06.542
across representations.

78
00:07:06.542 --> 00:07:14.308
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

79
00:07:14.426 --> 00:07:21.349
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

80
00:07:21.349 --> 00:07:21.844
analysis.

81
00:07:21.844 --> 00:07:30.813
Evaluation must match the task and the cost of
errors, while distribution shift, missing

82
00:07:30.813 --> 00:07:36.579
data, overfitting, and unexamined sampling
choices can invalidate conclusions.

83
00:07:36.579 --> 00:07:43.007
Consider this worked case: Students will
complete a checklist verifying data

84
00:07:43.007 --> 00:07:50.019
provenance, checking for distribution drift
between train and test sets, and documenting

85
00:07:50.019 --> 00:07:51.188
sampling methodology.

86
00:07:51.188 --> 00:07:59.082
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

87
00:07:59.082 --> 00:08:00.297
verification step.

88
00:08:00.297 --> 00:08:07.151
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

89
00:08:07.151 --> 00:08:07.641
intuition.
